1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,318
Three special quadrilaterals, and three surprisingly short lists of

2
00:00:03,318 --> 00:00:07,380
measurements. Each of them carries a property that supplies

3
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:08,891
whatever is missing.

4
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:13,609
Look at what each shape is given. A parallelogram:

5
00:00:13,609 --> 00:00:17,298
two sides and one angle. Only three measurements. A

6
00:00:17,298 --> 00:00:20,813
rhombus: two diagonals. Only two. A square: a single

7
00:00:20,813 --> 00:00:25,119
diagonal. Can that really be enough? It can, because

8
00:00:25,119 --> 00:00:27,693
the name of the shape is information too: its

9
00:00:27,693 --> 00:00:30,524
properties give us the measurements that were never listed.

10
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:33,937
Start with the parallelogram B E L T. We

11
00:00:33,937 --> 00:00:37,647
are given B E, four point two centimetres, E

12
00:00:37,647 --> 00:00:40,499
L, five centimetres, and the angle at T, forty

13
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:45,627
five degrees. In a parallelogram opposite angles are equal,

14
00:00:45,627 --> 00:00:48,279
so the angle at E is also forty five

15
00:00:48,279 --> 00:00:52,776
degrees. And consecutive angles are supplementary: one hundred and

16
00:00:52,776 --> 00:00:56,571
eighty minus forty five is one hundred and thirty

17
00:00:56,571 --> 00:00:59,517
five. So the angle at L is one hundred

18
00:00:59,517 --> 00:01:02,227
and thirty five degrees, and so is the angle

19
00:01:02,227 --> 00:01:05,798
at B. Four angles, from one given angle.

20
00:01:06,273 --> 00:01:10,430
Now the construction is straightforward. Draw B E, four

21
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:13,238
point two centimetres. At E set out forty five

22
00:01:13,238 --> 00:01:16,360
degrees and mark E L, five centimetres: that is

23
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,053
triangle B E L by side, angle, side. At

24
00:01:19,053 --> 00:01:22,822
B, set out one hundred and thirty five degrees

25
00:01:22,822 --> 00:01:24,713
and draw the ray. At L, set out one

26
00:01:24,713 --> 00:01:28,193
hundred and thirty five degrees as well, and draw

27
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:31,978
the second ray. The two rays meet at T,

28
00:01:31,978 --> 00:01:34,864
and the parallelogram is complete.

29
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,850
Now the rhombus. A rhombus is given by its

30
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:40,937
two diagonals: A C is four point five centimetres

31
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:43,320
and B D is six. Only two measurements, but

32
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:46,560
a rhombus has two properties we can use. Its

33
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:51,664
diagonals are perpendicular: they cross at right angles, at

34
00:01:51,664 --> 00:01:55,747
O. And they bisect each other, so O is

35
00:01:55,747 --> 00:01:58,408
the midpoint of both. Which means O B and

36
00:01:58,408 --> 00:02:01,691
O D are each half of six: three centimetres.

37
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:05,101
So this is how we construct it. Draw A

38
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:09,033
C, four point five centimetres, and mark its midpoint

39
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:13,461
O. Through O draw the perpendicular to A C:

40
00:02:13,461 --> 00:02:16,791
that is the line the other diagonal lies on.

41
00:02:16,791 --> 00:02:20,828
Set the compasses to three centimetres, put the needle

42
00:02:20,828 --> 00:02:24,733
at O, and mark the perpendicular on both sides.

43
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:26,733
Those marks are B and D, because each is

44
00:02:26,733 --> 00:02:30,643
three centimetres from O, exactly half of B D.

45
00:02:30,643 --> 00:02:32,884
Join A to B, B to C, C to

46
00:02:32,884 --> 00:02:35,893
D and D to A: the rhombus is complete,

47
00:02:35,893 --> 00:02:37,764
and we never needed its side lengths.

48
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:41,292
Here is a question worth asking: what if the

49
00:02:41,292 --> 00:02:44,340
two diagonals of the rhombus were equal? Watch the

50
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:47,760
shorter diagonal grow until both are the same length.

51
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:51,727
The rhombus becomes a square, because equal diagonals that

52
00:02:51,727 --> 00:02:55,248
bisect each other at right angles always give a

53
00:02:55,248 --> 00:02:57,833
square. So we can construct square J U M

54
00:02:57,833 --> 00:03:01,260
P with diagonal four point two centimetres the same

55
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:03,667
way: draw J U, find its midpoint, and draw

56
00:03:03,667 --> 00:03:08,183
the perpendicular through it. The other diagonal is equal,

57
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:10,680
so it is also four point two centimetres, and

58
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,180
O M and O P are two point one

59
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:15,407
centimetres each. Mark M and P, join the four

60
00:03:15,407 --> 00:03:17,151
vertices, and the square is complete.

61
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,969
Three shapes, three strategies. For the parallelogram, opposite angles

62
00:03:20,969 --> 00:03:23,449
are equal and consecutive angles add to one hundred

63
00:03:23,449 --> 00:03:26,494
and eighty degrees: that gives the missing angles. For

64
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:29,042
the rhombus, the diagonals bisect each other at right

65
00:03:29,042 --> 00:03:33,827
angles: that gives every vertex from the diagonals. For

66
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:36,360
the square the diagonals are equal as well: the

67
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:39,340
same construction, with one extra condition. So which property

68
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,191
gives the missing information?

69
00:03:41,793 --> 00:03:45,767
That is the method for every special quadrilateral. Identify

70
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:49,803
the shape. Recall its properties. Derive the measurements that

71
00:03:49,803 --> 00:03:54,486
are missing. Then construct. Analyse before you draw: the

72
00:03:54,486 --> 00:03:56,320
property tells you what to do with the ruler,

73
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:58,624
the compasses and the protractor.

74
00:03:59,107 --> 00:04:02,718
Your turn, two quick ones. A parallelogram has angle

75
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:07,048
A equal to sixty five degrees. What are the

76
00:04:07,048 --> 00:04:09,622
other three angles? And a rhombus has diagonals A

77
00:04:09,622 --> 00:04:11,955
C of eight centimetres and B D of six.

78
00:04:11,955 --> 00:04:15,207
How far is each vertex from the point where

79
00:04:15,207 --> 00:04:18,618
the diagonals cross? Pause the video and work them out.

80
00:04:23,677 --> 00:04:27,737
Let us check. In the parallelogram the angle opposite

81
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:30,437
A is C, so C is sixty five degrees;

82
00:04:30,437 --> 00:04:35,637
and the angles next to A are supplementary, so

83
00:04:35,637 --> 00:04:39,117
B and D are one hundred and eighty minus

84
00:04:39,117 --> 00:04:43,317
sixty five: one hundred and fifteen degrees each. In

85
00:04:43,317 --> 00:04:46,077
the rhombus, O is the midpoint of both diagonals,

86
00:04:46,077 --> 00:04:48,761
so O A and O C are four centimetres,

87
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:51,277
and O B and O D are three. Draw

88
00:04:51,277 --> 00:04:55,720
the perpendicular diagonals, mark those distances, and join the

89
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,028
ends: that is the rhombus.

90
00:04:57,627 --> 00:05:00,407
One idea holds all three together. A special shape

91
00:05:00,407 --> 00:05:04,158
comes with extra information: use its properties to find

92
00:05:04,158 --> 00:05:07,397
the missing measurements, and then construct. Know the shape.

93
00:05:07,397 --> 00:05:11,947
Use its properties. Construct it. Thank you for watching!

94
00:05:11,947 --> 00:05:13,338
See you in the next lesson!
